WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.850
LiteRev turns a research question into reviewed evidence and a predictive scenario. Here is a real one: avian influenza in people exposed at work. What the AI writes is a draft that a reviewer checks.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:30.200
Ask
Write the question in plain words or as a Boolean query. With live sources ticked, one search queries eleven databases, PubMed and OpenAlex among them.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:43.150
Every search becomes a scenario
Its records are deduplicated across sources and saved: 17,510 here, ready to be screened, synthesised and kept up to date.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:54.550
Score and select
Each record gets a semantic score and a Cohere rerank against the question. The threshold picks those that feed the analyses: 1,079 here. The rest are kept.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:08.950
The relevant articles, ranked
The relevant articles, ranked, beside each database's share and the years of publication. They export in six formats, from Excel and CSV to RIS and BibTeX.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:21.700
Read and decide
Open an article to read its abstract and its PICO, then include or exclude it with a reason. Each decision is counted in the PRISMA flow.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:36.650
Screen the PRISMA way
The PRISMA 2020 flow counts each step: 34,626 records from 11 databases, duplicates removed, AI pre-screening, human decisions and the evidence set.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:49.100
Database by database
Each database shows its count, the total it announced and whether a cap cut it. When one cannot take the whole Boolean query, the card gives the query it received.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:02:01.500
The search strategy, kept
The Strategy tab keeps the query as it was sent: the Boolean strategy, its PubMed version with MeSH terms and its explanation, ready for a methods section.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:13.150
Synthesise the whole corpus
Study designs, sources and levels of evidence are counted over all 1,079 relevant articles, in the database, never on a sample.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:25.450
Epidemiological parameters
Parameters such as R0, case fatality and incubation are pooled over every article that reports one, weighted by quality, with their interval and number of studies.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:38.600
An evidence brief, as a draft
The AI drafts a brief over the whole corpus, with a level of evidence and a GRADE rating, for a reviewer to check. It exports as a PDF or a citable report.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:56.150
Claims, actions and gaps
Each claim cites its articles; its strength comes from their designs, not from the model. Actions and research gaps follow, beside a gap matrix computed without AI.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:08.300
One extraction per article
Population, intervention, comparator and outcome are extracted once per article, then read across the whole corpus, never over a sample.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:24.600
Structured extraction
The Extraction tab fills the review's template from every relevant article, in full text when it is open: 1,076 articles, 17,489 values, each with its quote.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:37.850
What the papers report
These cards give the share of extracted articles that report each topic: sex, age, occupation, protective equipment, vaccination, animal hosts. A click lists them.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:50.150
Five sheets and a codebook
The values fill the template's five sheets, from human susceptibility to animals and vectors. Their labels follow a codebook a reviewer can edit.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:04:06.250
Every value has its quote
Open an article: each row gives a value, its cases and group size, and the exact sentence behind it, shown in the paper. A reviewer accepts, edits or rejects it.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:18.750
Take the extraction away
The extraction downloads as the review's Excel template, a flat CSV or a report in PDF or Word, and the reviewed rows as an annotated dataset.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:30.950
Where the evidence comes from
Each study's place resolves to a country and, when it is known, to a European NUTS region. Places that do not resolve are listed, never guessed.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:48.050
Pooled estimates and comparisons
Pooled estimates combine the counts the studies report, label by label, in a random-effects model with a forest plot. Comparisons give odds ratios between groups.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:59.390
Ask the corpus
The AI Assistant answers from the relevant articles only, with counts taken from the whole corpus. Each answer cites its sources and is kept with its date.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:12.290
Explore the literature
Topic clusters show what the corpus is made of. Each gets an AI summary, its key words and its most representative article, and its articles export.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:26.190
Map concepts and gaps
The concept map links pathogens, hosts, exposures, outcomes and places. Click one to see its links and articles. Pairs never studied together are gaps.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:38.090
From evidence to a model
The variables the literature points to become a model specification: an outcome, predictors with their sources and an algorithm. Plug in real data, and train.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:46.690
Keep it living
Subscribe to alerts. The living review queries PubMed on a schedule and adds the new articles to the scenario.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:59.640
Everything you can take away
Everything leaves the app: articles in six formats, the extraction workbook and its report, pooled estimates, the annotated dataset, clusters and concepts.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:08.040
This video is generated by code from the real interface, so it is rebuilt whenever the interface changes.
